ld to kill differing superheroes  and in fact that story was so popular  that marvel wrote a what if story where  what if wolverine had succeeded which  basically ended up becoming the story  that i call wolverine kills the marvel  universe but miller's contribution to  daredevil's rogues gallery didn't simply  stop there and although he didn't create  him miller is responsible for pitting  daredevil against his arch nemesis the  kingpin wilson fisk now prior to  daredevil 170 in 1981 when kingpin first  appeared in a daredevil comic he was  considered a spider-man villain and he  had originally debuted in the amazing  spider-man number 50 in 1967 but during  frank miller's run on daredevil not only  did miller give the kingpin a real name  in the form of wilson fisk but he made  kingpin first and foremost a daredevil  villain to the point that kingpin is  hardly associated with spider-man  anymore and since that time kingpin and  daredevil have been constantly locked in  a power struggle for control of hell's  kitchen and new york city as a whole  that's still playing out in the pages of  daredevil today but frank miller  ultimately left the title with issue  number 191 in 1983 but returned in 1986  to craft one of daredevil's most famous  story arcs and a story arc that is  actually sharing the name with daredevil  season 3 which is born again now during  this run matt murdock's ex-girlfriend  karen page resurfaces as a drug addict  he loses his law license after being  falsely set up by the kingpin and he  reunites with his mother who he believed  to have been dead but now discovers is  simply living as a nun and so one of the  things that i want to specify here is  that this particular story in a lot of  ways draws parallels with frank miller's  work the dark knight returns and despite  the fact that it was written three years  before the dark knight returns heavily  influenced frank miller's run on that  particular batman story but one of the  things that i also want to solidify here  is that frank miller's run on daredevil  is considered landmark because while it  did take away the significance of matt  murdock becoming who he was as a result  of what could arguably be considered  battling jack murdock's sacrifice in a  lot of ways it humanized the character  it brought daredevil down to a place  where this was a guy that struggled with  some of the most difficult things there  were times when he almost killed people  over the course of that story and in  fact following the death of elektra at  the hands of bullseye in frank miller's  run daredevil almost killed bullseye now  the way that played out just kind of a  quick explanation here the way that  played out is the fight really the kind  of final fight there between daredevil  and bullseye had actually been building  up for quite some time and it happened  several times over the course of frank  miller's run but the death of elektra at  the hands of bullseye at the hands of  lester was basically the final straw  right it was the the nail in the coffin  and that because of the fact that  daredevil had actually saved bullseye on  a multitude of occasions bullseye didn't  want to be saved and so he ultimately  tried to stab daredevil which led to  matt murdock letting bullseye fall and  then of course he ended up being  paralyzed now that leads to like a game  of russian roulette between the two of  them but the general point that i'm  making here is that where stan lee and  bill everett had focused heavily on what  you could argue to be a more  lighthearted version of daredevil frank  miller's run was very dark and very  visceral and that's why people look at  it as the defining run that laid the  groundwork for everything we know about  daredevil as we see him written today  but following frank miller's second exit  from the book a year later daredevil  basically went through a few different  writers most notably ann necenti who  wrote the title for four and a half  y