 face of marvel comics  but guys like roy thomas and guys like  marv wolfman and people like jerry  conway frank miller and chris claremont  all had massive contributions but one of  the funny things is a lot of the work  that chris claremont did in his handling  of the x-men title focusing on turning  it into a soap opera as opposed to just  focusing on the pretty white kids with  problems type of storytelling that stan  lee jack kirby arnold drake and roy  thomas focused on is that it was heavily  influenced by the work of frank miller  his work on daredevil influenced the  work of chris claremont and the reason  for this is one of the first things that  frank miller did was make huge changes  to daredevil's origin instead of being a  down on his luck fighter that had turned  to fixing fights out of desperation to  support his son battling jack murdock  was depicted as an abusive alcoholic now  frank miller had also introduced the  character of stick who was a blind  martial arts master that had trained  daredevil shortly before he lost a sight  in daredevil issue 176. now one of the  things that i want to point out here is  that while we look back at frank  miller's writing in hindsight and say  this is definitively the single greatest  daredevil run that there's ever been it  was not without controversy one of the  big controversies was the adjustment  that was made to battling jack murdock  that at the time people saw battling  jack murdock as a guy who fixed fights  and that one of the things people loved  the most about battling jack murdock's  death was the fact that he died as a  result of refusing to fix fights which  basically showed that people could be  redeemed that was again heavily  influential on matt murdock in the idea  that not everybody was without saving  when it came to frank miller changing  that and not just altering that origin  of battling jack but actually turning  him into a guy that basically beat on  matt murdock himself there was a subsect  of the daredevil fan base that holy  rejected it they absolutely despised  that adjustment to the character but  another change that frank miller had  made is that he introduced daredevil's  most iconic supporting character in the  form of elektra nachios in daredevil  issue 168 the first issue that he  started on now since her introduction  elektra has been used as an ally an  enemy and a love interest at various  times and she would become so popular  during the frank miller run that she  would actually star in her own solo  series shortly after debuting and met  matt murdock at columbia university and  the two began dating but after she and  her father are kidnapped and her  father's killed elektra goes to china  and she meets stick who trains her and  tries to neutralize the darkness that's  inside of her but she ends up joining an  ancient sect of evil known as the hand  now the hand was another huge  introduction during frank miller's run  and their significance cannot be  overstated now the hand which debuted in  daredevil issue 174 are a centuries-old  clan of ninja assassins that are devoted  to the service of an entity known as the  great evil beast now stick who of course  again was the guy that trained both  daredevil and elektra leads a group  known as the cast which is basically his  own kind of antithesis to the hand  themselves in order to face off and  defeat the hand now although elektra  does eventually defect from the hand and  then battle them alongside daredevil the  hand has been one of daredevil's  fiercest and most frequent foes ever  since their introduction and in fact the  hand has historically been so popular in  marvel comics that other characters from  marvel were rolled over to be involved  with the hand a really good example of  this was the enemy of the state  storyline with wolverine where wolverine  was basically kidnapped by the hand and  hydra working together in order to  brainwash him and then send him out into  the world to kill differing superheroes  and in fact that story was so popular  that marvel wrote a what if story where  what if wolverine had succeeded which  basically ended up becoming the story  that i call wolverine kills the marvel  universe but miller's contribution to  daredevil's rogues gallery didn't simply  stop there and although he didn't create  him miller is responsible for pitting  daredevil against his arch nemesis the  kingpin wilson fisk now prior to  daredevil 170 in 1981 when kingpin first  appeared in a daredevil comic he was  considered a spider-man villain and he  had originally debuted in the amazing  spider-man number 50 in 1967 but during  frank miller's run on daredevil not only  did miller give the kingpin a real name  in the form of wilson fisk but he made  kingpin first and foremost a daredevil  villain to the point that kingpin is  hardly associated with spider-man  anymore and since that time kingpin and  daredevil have been constantly locked in  a power struggle for control of hell's  kitchen and new york city as a whole  that's still playing out in the pages of  daredevil today but frank miller  ultimately left the title with issue  number 191 in 1983 but returned in 1986  to craft one of daredevil's most famous  story arcs and a story arc that is  actually sharing the name with daredevil  season 3 which is born again now during  this run matt murdock's ex-girlfriend  karen page resurfaces as a drug addict  he loses his law license after being  falsely set up by the kingpin and he  reunites with his mother who he believed  to have been dead but now discovers is  simply living as a nun and so one of the  things that i want to specify here is  that this particular story in a lot of  ways draws parallels with frank miller's  work the dark knight returns and despite  the fact that it was written three years  before the dark knight returns heavily  influenced frank miller's run on that  particular batman story but one of the  things that i also want to solidify here  is that frank miller's run on daredevil  is considered landmark because while it  did take away the significance of matt  murdock becoming who he was as a result  of what could arguably be considered  battling jack murdock's sacrifice in a  lot of ways it humanized the character  it brought daredevil down to a place  where this was a guy that struggled with  some of the most difficult things there  were times when he almost killed people  over the course of that story and in  fact following the death of elektra at  the hands of bullseye in frank miller's  run daredevil almost killed bullseye now  the way that played out just kind of a  quick explanation here the way that  played out is the fight really the kind  of final fight there between daredevil  and bullseye had actually been building  up for quite some time and it happened  several times over the course of frank  miller's run but the death of elektra at  the hands of bullseye at the hands of  lester was basically the final straw  right it was the the nail in the coffin  and that because of the fact that  daredevil had actually saved bullseye on  a multitude of occasions bullseye didn't  want to be saved and so he ultimately  tried to stab daredevil which led to  matt murdock letting bullseye fall and  then of course he ended up being  paralyzed now that leads to like a game  of russian roulette between the two of  them but the general point that i'm  making here is that where stan lee and  bill everett had focused heavily on what  you could argue to be a more  lighthearted version of daredevil frank  miller's run was very dark and very  visceral and that's why people look at  it as the defining run that laid the  groundwork for everything we know about  daredevil as we see him written today  but following frank miller's second exit  from the book a year later daredevil  basically went through a few different  writers most notably ann necenti who  wrote the title for four and a half  years from 1987 to 1991. now frank  miller would revisit the character in  the 1983 mini-series daredevil the man  without fear alongside artist john  romita jr but the biggest development  post miller would actually come in 1998  when the daredevil title was rebooted  during the marvel knights publishing  initiative now this is something that  we've talked about before over the  course of marvel comics but i think it's  worth repeating here that while we don't  have to necessarily go into the  backstory as to why it happened the  reality is that in the mid-1990s marvel  was basically going bankrupt and so  marvel did a couple different things in  order to keep themselves from completely  going broke one of the first things they  did is they started licensing off the  film rights to characters that they  owned hence the reason why sony owned  the rights to the spider-man films for  so long and why fox owned the fantastic  four and the x-men but the thing about  this is what they also did is they  launched marvel knights now marvel  knights was an imprint that was actually  handed off from marvel comics to a  company called event publications which  was ran by jimmy palmiotti and joe  quesada and the way this worked is that  instead of marvel publishing these  stories in-house with their own writers  and their own artists which was an  incredibly expensive venture given the  fact that people wanted to be paid top  dollar that they could actually license  it out to event publications which would  have a cheaper roster of writers and  artists that would basically write  characters that marvel couldn't really  sell for a whole ton of money but made  some kind of money in effect event  publications was right in the characters  that marvel just couldn't justify paying  top dollar to create and so that's where  marvel knights came from but with joe  casada and paul miati handling the  penciling and the inking filmmaker kevin  smith was tapped to write the initial  daredevil story arc under the marvel  knights imprint and so while kevin smith  only wrote one eight issue arc of  daredevil the story arc guardian devil  remains a fan favorite and in this arc  which is an incredibly dark tone in a  lot of ways is a return to frank miller  this explored matt murdock's complicated  relationship with his catholic faith as  daredevil was forced to take care of an  infant that could either be a new  messiah or the antichrist now of course  kevin smith's story also sees the death  of karen page at the hands of bullseye  but this basically led us into the early  2000s when daredevils enjoyed some of  the most consistent good writing of any  marvel title with several critically  acclaimed runs on the character starting  with brian michael bendis taking over  the title with issue number 16 in 2001  and continuing until issue number 81 in  2006. now venice's run is still seen as  one of the best on daredevil due in  large part to the art of dave mack and  alex malev and an intensification of the  rivalry with kingpin which ultimately  sees daredevil's secret identity exposed  to the world but bendis's successor eric  brubaker stayed on the title until 2009  and his run was also a hit with critics  and fans alike now in 2012 writer mark  wade and artist chris samny took over  the title but by this time because  comics were consistently undoing things  that had basically been done daredevil's  identity is secret once again and he's  basically regained his law license  however by the end of the run he's  basically forced to reveal himself as  matt murdock yet again which basically  results in the state of new york  disbarring him which prompts matt  murdock to move across the country to  san diego now one of the things that i  want to specify here is that the reason  why things were being changed between  2010 and 2011 is because what you had in  this 10-year window between 2000 and  2010 is you had what was called the joe  casada era which has basically been met  by a combination of revile and fan  celebration it really depends on who  you're talking to and it depends on what  particular comic you're talking about  but joe casada coming on as  editor-in-chief in 2000 was basically  his reward for helping keeping marvel  afloat by having or by launching event  publications alongside jimmy palmiotti  and so that's why with jokasada's run  what you saw was the first five years as  editor-in-chief literally just breaking  everything down which was essentially  him going through and dismantling things  so getting rid of the marriage between  peter parker and mary jane watson and  spider-man one more day getting rid of  98  of the mutant population during house of  m initiating the superhero civil war the  death of captain america different  things along those lines and then for  the second half of that 10-year run it  was the rebuilding phase masai complex  messiah wars hope summers and the 10  lights all of which happen in the x-men  comics the launch of new avengers  different things along those lines but  in 2010 due to a series of controversies  specifically in captain america joe  casada was actually removed as  editor-in-chief he was elevated up to  chief creative officer so essentially  they said you can help with ideas but  you can't actually do anything and then  in his place as editor-in-chief came  axel alonso which actually initiated  something called the heroic age and the  heroic age is basically where everything  that people loved about jokasada's run  was continued on and everything that  people hated about jokasada's run was  basically eliminated the only real  exception being the marriage between  peter parker and mary jane watson but  having said all of that getting back  into daredevil itself i felt like that  kind of bit of history needed to be  explored matt murdock would ultimately  return to new york in 2015 under the  writing of charles soule who took over  the title as part of marvel's all-new  all different marvel initiative which  was one of several attempts by marvel to  boost sales and fan interest by  switching around creative teams but none  of which really had a cohesive direction  like marvel knights had and so  daredevil's identity would again become  secret once again thanks to the children  of the purple man who actually inherited  their father's ability to manipulate the  minds of others and use it to force  people to forget daredevil is matt  murdock but later in charles soule's run  matt murdock ends up finding himself as  deputy mayor of the city of new york and  the full-time mayor is none other than  wilson fisk but when fisk is attacked by  the hand matt has to assume the role of  mayor temporarily now the reason why  this happened this is actually one of  the cooler elements of marvel comics is  what you have was a story called secret  empire which is still one of my favorite  stories of all time but in effect the  red skull has gotten his hands on a  cosmic cube and used it to alter the  entire backstory of captain america so  that instead of steve rogers being a guy  who really wanted to fight against the  axis powers in world war ii getting the  super soldier serum and the rest just  simply being history that instead he had  basically always been a hydra agent and  so when he became captain america he had  secretly been working behind the scenes  to orchestrate a takeover of hydra which  he successfully did they actually ended  up conquering north america and then he  had his side set on the rest of the  world but during this story one of the  ways in which he basically started  removing superheroes from basically his  path to taking over north america  is he had a villain basically blanket  the city of new york and dark force  energy essentially new york was just cut  off from the rest of the world and  encased in darkness and so while you did  have the superhero specifically dagger  who was a superhero that had the ability  to emanate light while you did have her  alongside the other superheroes trying  to find a way out wilson fisk was  actually the one on the ground level  keeping the average person safe from  just general petty criminals and other  criminals who were going through and  trying to capitalize on the lawlessness  and so that's why when secret empire  came to an end wilson fisk was elected  as mayor because the people saw him as  their actual champion as opposed to the  avengers none of whom were protecting  them from just ground level criminals  and so at the moment the current writer  of daredevil is chip zadarski who took  over the title in 2019. now zadarski's  run actually started with daredevil  accidentally beating a criminal so badly  that he killed him and then he  subsequently turns himself over to the  cops and then basically goes to prison  but not as matt murdock but as the  costume vigilante daredevil and so with  matt murdock in prison elektra ends up  taking up the mantle of daredevil and  defends hell's kitchen from crime and  does so according to matt's code of no  killing which of course is at odds with  her long-standing history as an assassin  now recently matt murdock's basically  been released from prison and both he  and elektra currently don the daredevil  costumes and simply fight crime side by  side but that's where we are with the  daredevil comics here in 2022 it's kind  of continuing that trend now how long  that's gonna go for we don't entirely  know and again there are some things  that i left out here for example there  was a point where daredevil's uh twin  brother was basically revealed or at  least had been accidentally created by  an inhuman that daredevil had actually  created an identity for himself at one  point in time but this inhuman not  realizing that he basically read this  journal of daredevil and then brought  matt murdock's alter ego to life and  then passed him off or at least matt  passed him off as his twin brother that  was ultimately undone but again you know  there were some things that had happened  that were a little questionable which is  normal when it comes to chemical  characters but with that being said guys  we're gonna bring this video to an end  you are now an expert on daredevil  go forth and join the conversations on  on matt murdock's character but thank  you all for watching and i will catch  you all later peace
 

